WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.707 --> 00:00:03.124
(rock music)

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:32.033
- We ready to go?

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:34.110
You guys rolling?

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.170
(laughter)

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:36.584
Oh, this is hard.

00:00:36.584 --> 00:00:39.000
(rock music)

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.780
So how is this course built?

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:42.530
There are basically 3 main sections

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:43.540
to this course.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:45.060
The first one covers my current

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.800
animation work flow.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.470
How I structure my videos,

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:49.800
plan my stories,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.770
create the layout animatic,

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:53.580
work with other animators,

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.710
the actual animation process,

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.320
how I produce the backgrounds,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:58.740
and different assets,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.720
visual effects,

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.210
and sound design.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:02.510
This section takes you through each

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:03.530
of the steps we go through

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.200
when we make our animations
from start to finish.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.080
In the next section,

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.991
I actually create an
entire 30 second long video

00:01:09.991 --> 00:01:12.290
in the spirit of the original

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:14.098
animator vs animation video

00:01:14.098 --> 00:01:16.400
and over the course of 20 lessons,

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.050
I show you the entire process

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:19.610
in the actual software

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:20.900
from creating a new file,

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.210
to exporting the final animation.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:26.360
This is by far the most
detailed inside look

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.380
I've ever posted about
my animation process.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:30.520
In the last section,

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.730
I talk a bit about my personal journey

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:35.460
of becoming an animator, what it's like,

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:36.700
and what you should expect

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.500
if you're interested in it as a career.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.880
If you want to become
an animator on YouTube,

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.336
focus on your storytelling,

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:45.030
not your animation quality.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.250
Improving your animation
quality is always great,

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.576
but what engages people

00:01:48.576 --> 00:01:50.310
is the story that you're telling.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.000
(rock music)

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.080
Being an animator is a constant cycle

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.650
of delayed gratification

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:58.260
where you work hard for a long time,

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.550
until you see a big reward at the end

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:01.910
and I wouldn't trade this job

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:03.410
for any other job in the world.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:05.100
My name is Alan Becker,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:08.413
and welcome to Stick
Figure Animation Course.

